# Baseline for dev/check_dead_helpers.py — the dropped-call-site audit.
#
# Format: `path_glob::symbol_glob  # reason`. Every entry MUST carry a reason; the
# script refuses to run otherwise.
#
# What this audit catches: a helper upstream calls from library code that, here, is
# referenced only from tests — or from nothing. That is the signature of **helper
# landed, call site dropped**, the one failure shape all five other checks are blind
# to by construction (the file is present, the `def` is present, nothing was
# deleted, and the missing hunk is in a different, usually already-allowlisted file).
#
# Two kinds of entry belong here, and they are NOT the same thing:
#
#   REVIEWED — established that the helper is intentionally unreachable in this
#     fork (superseded by fork work, or upstream's caller is code we deliberately
#     do not have). Say what you established.
#
#   CONFIRMED-DROPPED — established that it IS a real dropped call site, with the
#     restore tracked and pending. This is a **debt marker, not approval.** It keeps
#     the check gating for everything else instead of being switched off wholesale.
#     Every entry of this kind must name the owning commit.
#
# The stale-entry warning is what stops CONFIRMED-DROPPED entries from rotting: the
# moment a call site is restored, its entry stops excusing anything and the check
# says so.
#
# Validated to fire before being trusted: reverting `server/app.py`'s restored
# `apc_disk_namespace` call site makes it report 4 hits and exit 1; restoring it
# drops back to 3. Runs in ~10s.

